 foods uh you know  very Bare Bones diet but then you would  systematically reintroduce foods and try  to figure out how do I work back to a  sustainable long-term dietary pattern  that doesn't aggravate my food allergies  or sensitivities you'd want to do that  under the direction of a qualified  professional  um but you know when we talk about these  really restrictive diets you know maybe  if you're doing an Elimination Diet you  might have a super restrictive pattern  for a couple weeks at a time but this  idea that you're just going to eat  ground beef for the rest of your life is  is really not a suitable solution even  in that particular application  um one other thing I want to mention  with the carnivore diet  I just think this is interesting and I  haven't heard anyone really talk about  it there's a lot of chatter in the  carnivore world that you know um first  of all you know these carnivore diets  are are totally  um you know if you're eating nose to  tail as they always say uh you know  you're good to go plenty of  micronutrients this is how you know  plenty of hunter-gatherer populations  would get their micronutrients is by  eating you know eyeballs and skin and  things like that I did come across a  paper where they were looking at dietary  patterns in East Greenland uh in a  hunter-gatherer population and they're  looking at data from 1936 to 1937 and  how this hunter-gatherer population was  eating and more specifically they were  looking at how they were getting vitamin  C in an area where there really wasn't a  lot of what we would call terrestrial  vegetation not a lot of land-based  plants that you could grow and eat for  vitamin C content and so I'm just going  to read this quote from from the from  the paper the main conclusions of the  present study are the important role of  algae consumption in greenlandic  traditional dietary pattern to avoid  scurvy and that Foods traditionally seen  as important sources of vitamin C like  ice from Seals and narwhal skin played a  minor role in meeting the vitamin C  requirements so basically what they did  was they reanalyzed some of this  nutritional data from several decades  ago and said you know we always assumed  that that these individuals were getting  most of their vitamin C from seal  eyeballs and getting it from narwhal  skin one of the things that they were  talking about in the paper is  I mean if you're getting a lot if this  group of individuals was getting a huge  portion of their vitamin C from seal  eyeballs that is a lot of seals that you  need to be able to to hunt uh over a  given time frame and so one of the  things that tipped them off into looking  more deeply into algae consumption was  they were just like I mean from a  feasibility perspective how are you  getting this many C uh seal eyeballs  um you know in order to sustain the  dietary needs of this population of  hunter-gatherer so  um I think in some cases uh there's a a  tendency to assume that yeah this nose  to tail thing is how everybody does it  no one no one in their right mind would  be eating vegetation whether it's sea  vegetation or terrestrial vegetation see  when I say sea vegetation I mean you  know algae seaweed things like that  um so I just wanted to highlight in uh  what I found to be an interesting  observation which is that even in  populations that have traditionally been  assumed to be almost fully  carnivorous in terms of their dietary  pattern it's very possible that they  have been leaning on things like seaweed  and algae for some micronutrient  coverage more than we might anticipate  now big big caveat  I have just opened myself up to a world  of pain I have no interest in going Toe  to Toe with uh with someone whose entire  life is built around the carnivore diet  digging into this kind of population  level research about who which of these  hunter-gatherer populations is you know  fully carnivorous and which ones are not  um that that's not something that  